<div class="container">

<p>Hi all,</p>

<p>As agreed, I'd like to share a detailed overview of the Device Credential Broker solution
we are building to securely delegate the Windows computer-rename operation, with particular
focus on how device authentication and authorization are enforced. The public repository is
available at:
<a href="https://github.com/robgrame/RenameCredentialBrokerApi">https://github.com/robgrame/RenameCredentialBrokerApi</a>
(full documentation under <code>/docs</code>).</p>

<h1>1. Problem Statement</h1>
<p>The existing Hybrid Join rename workflow executes <code>Rename-Computer</code> locally on the
endpoint, using a domain account whose permissions are not always sufficient and which must be
present on every machine. The new requirement is to remove the rename logic from the endpoint
entirely and delegate it to a dedicated broker service that:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Retrieves a privileged, rotating service-account credential from CyberArk server-side only;</li>
  <li>Releases it to a requesting device only after that device has been strongly authenticated and explicitly authorized;</li>
  <li>Never allows the endpoint to talk to CyberArk directly.</li>
</ul>

<h1>2. High-Level Trust Model</h1>
<pre>Windows Device
  &rarr; Device Certificate (mTLS)
  &rarr; Credential Broker (.NET 10 / ASP.NET Core)
  &rarr; Device Validation (Microsoft Graph / Entra ID / Intune)
  &rarr; Operation Authorization
  &rarr; CyberArk Credential Retrieval (server-to-server only)
  &rarr; Credential returned to the authorized device
  &rarr; PSCredential &rarr; Rename-Computer -DomainCredential</pre>

<p>The core design principle is: <strong>possession of the API URL (or even a valid certificate)
is never, by itself, sufficient</strong>. A request must pass every one of the following
independent gates before the broker will even consider contacting CyberArk. Each gate fails
closed &mdash; any error, timeout, or ambiguous result is treated as a denial, never as an
implicit "allow."</p>

<h1>3. Device Authentication &amp; Authorization - Detailed Pipeline</h1>
<p>This is implemented as a strict sequence of independent checks (source:
<code>ComputerRenameEndpoints.cs</code> and supporting classes). Every request must pass all of
them, in order:</p>

<div class="step">
  <strong>Step 1 &mdash; Mutual TLS (transport layer)</strong><br>
  The device presents its machine certificate during the TLS handshake. Requests without a valid
  client certificate never reach the application layer.
</div>

<div class="step">
  <strong>Step 2 &mdash; Request signature &amp; anti-replay</strong><br>
  The device signs the raw request body with the private key of its certificate. The broker
  (<code>SignedRequestAuthenticator</code>) verifies this signature, then
  (<code>ReplayProtector</code>) checks timestamp freshness (default max clock skew: 300 seconds)
  and atomically reserves the (certificate-thumbprint, nonce) pair in a nonce store, rejecting any
  reuse. Only after this step succeeds does the broker even parse the JSON payload.
</div>

<div class="step">
  <strong>Step 3 &mdash; Certificate trust validation</strong> (<code>DeviceCertificateValidator</code>)<br>
  Depending on the configured trust profile (<code>IntuneEnrollment</code> or
  <code>CorporatePKI</code>): certificate validity window, required Enhanced Key Usage (Client
  Authentication), issuer allow-list, full chain build, with the root certificate checked against
  an explicit, configured trust-anchor list (never implicit OS trust-store membership). If no
  trust anchor is configured at all, the service denies by design rather than "trusting everything."
</div>

<div class="step">
  <strong>Step 4 &mdash; Device identity resolution</strong> (<code>DeviceIdentityResolver</code>)<br>
  The device's unique identifier (GUID) is extracted exclusively from the certificate itself
  &mdash; from the SAN URI (<code>urn:uuid:...</code>), SAN DNS entry, or Subject CN &mdash;
  requiring an exact, whole-value match. Partial/substring matching is deliberately rejected, as
  it is a known IDOR vector. Any value the client reports in the JSON body (serial number,
  current hostname) is kept separate and is never treated as trusted on its own.
</div>

<div class="step">
  <strong>Step 5 &mdash; Anti-IDOR cross-check</strong><br>
  If the request body claims an Entra device ID, it must match exactly the device ID derived from
  the certificate in Step 4. Any mismatch is logged as a <code>SuspiciousCredentialRequest</code>
  and denied &mdash; this prevents Device A from requesting a credential while impersonating
  Device B.
</div>

<div class="step">
  <strong>Step 6 &mdash; Anti-duplicate request claim</strong><br>
  The request ID is atomically claimed in the credential lease store
  (<code>TryClaimRequestIdAsync</code>) &mdash; not a "check then act," which would leave a race
  window where two concurrent requests with the same ID could both pass.
</div>

<div class="step">
  <strong>Step 7 &mdash; Directory validation against Microsoft Graph</strong>
  (<code>CompositeDeviceDirectoryValidator</code>)<br>
  Delegates to two independent, composable validators:
  <ul>
    <li><code>EntraDeviceDirectoryValidator</code>: confirms the device exists in Entra ID and is enabled.</li>
    <li><code>IntuneManagedDeviceValidator</code>: confirms Intune management state and, where policy requires it, compliance state.</li>
  </ul>
  These requirements are configuration-driven (<code>RequireEntraDevice</code>,
  <code>RequireIntuneManagedDevice</code>, <code>RequireCompliantDevice</code>), so policy can be
  tightened per environment without code changes. Any Graph error or outage is treated as a
  denial &mdash; never converted into an authorization success.
</div>

<div class="step">
  <strong>Step 8 &mdash; Operation authorization</strong> (<code>DeviceOperationAuthorizationService</code>)<br>
  Confirms the requested operation is supported (currently only <code>ComputerRename</code>), that
  directory validation succeeded, and queries an abuse-detection component
  (<code>IAbuseDetector</code>) that rate-limits requests per device (default: 3 requests /
  10-minute window) and flags anomalous patterns.
</div>

<div class="step">
  <strong>Step 9 &mdash; Credential retrieval</strong><br>
  Only once all eight preceding gates have succeeded does the broker contact CyberArk
  server-to-server to retrieve the current, rotating credential for the pre-configured
  computer-rename service account. The device never selects the CyberArk Safe, object, or account
  name &mdash; it only ever says "authorize me for ComputerRename," and the mapping to the actual
  credential is entirely server-side configuration. A short-lived credential lease (no password
  stored) is then issued to correlate the request with the eventual rename-result report, without
  persisting the secret itself.
</div>

<h1>4. Residual Risk (Documented Transparently)</h1>
<p>Because the current requirement is to return the password to the endpoint for
<code>Rename-Computer -DomainCredential</code>, the architecture does <strong>not</strong>
eliminate the theoretical risk of credential capture from process memory by an attacker with
sufficient SYSTEM-level control over the endpoint. This is documented explicitly (see the ADR
comparing "return password to endpoint" vs. "perform the rename via a trusted server-side
mechanism without exposing the password") as a decision to revisit in a future hardening phase.</p>

<h1>5. Status</h1>
<p>The solution is implemented in .NET 10, fully tested (unit/integration/security test suites,
all currently green), and documented end-to-end under <code>/docs</code> (architecture,
authentication, device-validation, CyberArk integration, threat model, ADRs). Open items still
requiring validation with the CyberArk/PKI/Entra teams are tracked explicitly in the docs as
"REQUIRES CUSTOMER VALIDATION."</p>

